## The Compiler Prevents Double Free

A **double free** occurs when the same memory is freed twice — a critical bug in C/C++. XIOM's ownership system makes double-free impossible.

### Why Double Free is Dangerous

```c
// C code (dangerous)
// void* ptr = malloc(100);
// free(ptr);
// free(ptr);  // DOUBLE FREE — undefined behavior, crash, or security hole
```

### How XIOM Prevents It

```xiom
let v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(42);
// v owns the Vec
// v is dropped exactly once — when it goes out of scope
// No manual free — no possibility of double free
```

### Move Prevents Double Free

```xiom
let a = Vec[Int]();
a.push(1);
let b = a;  // ownership MOVES
// a is dead — won't be dropped
// b will be dropped — only one drop
// No double free possible
```

### mem.drop vs Automatic Drop

```xiom
let v = Vec[Int]();
v.push(1);
mem.drop(v);  // manual drop — v is dead
// v won't be dropped again at end of scope
// Double free impossible
```

### Clone Creates Independent Values

```xiom
let a = Vec[Int]();
a.push(1);
let b = a.clone();  // new allocation
// a and b are independent — each dropped once
// No double free
```

### The Guarantee

Every value in XIOM is dropped **exactly once**. The compiler tracks ownership and ensures no value is:
- Dropped twice (double free)
- Not dropped at all (memory leak)
- Used after being dropped (use-after-free)

